# pages that do not depend on the lanes' findings: cover, goals, theme, ui types
from common import *
INVADER=[ "00100000100","00010001000","00111111100","01101110110","11111111111","10111111101","10100000101","00011011000"]
def invader(px=5,color=INK):
    cells="".join(f'<rect x="{x*px}" y="{y*px}" width="{px}" height="{px}" fill="{color}"/>' for y,row in enumerate(INVADER) for x,c in enumerate(row) if c=="1")
    return f'<svg width="{11*px}" height="{8*px}" viewBox="0 0 {11*px} {8*px}" style="vertical-align:middle">{cells}</svg>'
cover=f'''<div style="height:100%;display:flex;flex-direction:column;justify-content:space-between">
<div><div class="mono eb">Research · {DATE}</div>
<h1 style="font-size:40pt;max-width:155mm">What the best growing games do, and what a gym app can borrow.</h1>
<p class="lead" style="margin-top:8mm;max-width:145mm">Twenty-two growing, tending and walking games read on one day: what grows, what happens when you stop, what you earn and spend, how much is on the screen, and what their players complain about. Written for the RepGarden game-design stage, where the vegetable garden gets its rules.</p></div>
<div style="display:flex;flex-wrap:wrap;gap:3mm;max-width:160mm">{"".join(icon(s,38,0) for s in ALL)}</div>
<div><div class="mono">Prepared by</div><div class="wm" style="font-size:18pt">gadirlabs<b>.</b></div><div class="small">Ahmed Gadir · gadirlabs.io · Store figures are the GB App Store on {DATE}; mechanics are from each game's own pages, help centre or wiki; complaints are quoted from reviews and Reddit with a source for each. Anything that could not be confirmed is marked.</div></div></div>'''
goals=f'''<div class="mono eb">Why this report</div><h2>The questions the garden has to answer.</h2>
<p>RepGarden's research found that none of the fourteen trainer platforms has a character, a world, or anything that grows: their game layers are streaks, badges and leaderboards bolted on with no theme. RepGarden's theme is decided, vegetables and growth, and this stage turns it into rules. Before inventing them, we read the games that already make millions of people tend something, and the habit apps that grow a plant or a creature out of real-life behaviour.</p>
<div class="grid2" style="margin-top:4mm"><div><h3>The box the game is designed inside</h3>
<table class="dense"><tr><th>Constraint</th><th>What it means for the design</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>The client plays, not the trainer</b></td><td>Every game event is derived from what the trainer already does and what the client already logs. The trainer gets no new work.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>The venue is the gym</b></td><td>One hand, a glance of a few seconds between sets; a longer sit-down after the workout and in the evening. Never a locked-in session.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>The game only sees what the app records</b></td><td>Sessions done against sessions planned, sets logged, check-ins, personal bests. Not effort, not form.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Rest days are prescribed</b></td><td>Clients train two to five times a week. Anything daily punishes the rest the trainer asked for.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Free, no shop</b></td><td>No paid currency, so the economy can be honest: everything earned is earned by training.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>One simple scene</b></td><td>Full-width controls, no drill-down menus, tasteful rather than busy.</td></tr></table></div>
<div><h3>What we needed to learn</h3>
<p class="dim" style="font-size:10pt">For every game, the same thirteen questions: what is the unit of play; what does a session look like; what happens when you stop coming and how you recover; what you earn, what it buys and whether that feeds back; stakes, risk and chance; progression; social; freedom and identity; the one signature interaction; the UI type and how dense the screen is; sound and feel; how big it is and what players say; and what transfers to a gym app.</p>
<h3>The decisions this report feeds, in order</h3>
<table class="dense"><tr><th>#</th><th>Decision</th><th>Page</th></tr>
<tr><td>1</td><td>The story: is the client the plant, or the gardener?</td><td>4</td></tr>
<tr><td>2</td><td>The unit of play: what in the training grows what in the garden</td><td>5</td></tr>
<tr><td>3</td><td>Absence: what withers, how fast, and how it is recovered</td><td>6</td></tr>
<tr><td>4</td><td>The economy: what you earn and what it buys</td><td>7</td></tr>
<tr><td>5</td><td>The screen: which UI type fits a glance between sets</td><td>8</td></tr>
<tr><td>6</td><td>Surprise, risk, social and freedom: which mechanics, and when</td><td>9</td></tr>
<tr><td>7</td><td>The MVP cut, and what to avoid</td><td>10</td></tr></table>
<div class="call" style="margin-top:4mm;font-size:9.5pt">Page 11 is the theming argument, written for the video. Pages 12 onwards are the evidence: the games one by one, their size, and what their players say. The decisions themselves are recorded in the RepGarden repository, not here.</div></div></div>'''
theme=f'''<div class="mono eb">Why a theme at all</div><h2>Shapes with a story win.</h2>
<div class="grid3">
<div class="card"><div class="n">{invader(4,VIO)}<span style="margin-left:3mm">Space Invaders, 1978</span></div><div class="d">Mechanically it is a row of shapes descending on a shape that shoots. Taito drew the shapes as aliens, called the shooter a laser cannon and the shields bunkers, and the same mechanics became an invasion you are holding back. The occasional saucer across the top is the risk: leave your lane to shoot it and the rows get closer.</div></div>
<div class="card"><div class="n">{icon("chess",26)}Chess</div><div class="d">A grid, six movement rules. The medieval theme, kings, knights, castles and a church, gives every piece a reason for how it moves and makes a trade feel like a sacrifice. People have cared about the outcome of a board of shapes for a thousand years because the shapes have names.</div></div>
<div class="card"><div class="n">{icon("fruitninja",26)}Fruit Ninja, 2010</div><div class="d">Swipe to cut moving targets, avoid some of them. The theme chose the gesture: a blade through fruit, with the juice splash and the slash mark as the feedback. The mechanic and the theme are the same thing, which is why it is still on phones fifteen years later.</div></div></div>
<p style="margin-top:5mm">The trainer platforms RepGarden competes with have the mechanics, points, streaks, leaderboards, and no theme, so a streak is a number and a badge is a shape. The games in this report are the other way round: in every one, the thing on the screen is something you would recognise from life, a farm, a tree, a bird, a brain, and the mechanics are explained by it. Nobody reads a manual for Plant Nanny. You drink water, the plant drinks water.</p>
<div class="grid2"><div><h3>What a theme does for the mechanics</h3>
<p class="dim" style="font-size:10pt"><b>It explains the rules.</b> A plant that wilts when it is not watered needs no tutorial; a "streak at risk" banner does. <b>It sets the emotion.</b> Growth, tending, harvest, seasons: the garden says what the app wants the client to feel without a line of copy. <b>It picks the gesture.</b> Fruit gets sliced, a plant gets watered, a set gets logged: the theme should decide what the one tap between sets looks like. <b>It makes a loss bearable.</b> A wilted bed is sad and fixable; a streak reset to zero is a verdict.</p></div>
<div><h3>Why vegetables fit the gym</h3>
<p class="dim" style="font-size:10pt">Vegetables grow slowly, visibly and on their own schedule, and they grow most when you are not looking. So do muscles: the work is the session, the growth happens in the rest between. That is one true sentence that is both a story and a training principle, and it is the sentence the whole garden can be built on: <b>the workout is the watering; the growth comes on the rest day.</b></p>
<div class="call" style="font-size:9.5pt;margin-top:3mm">The name already carries it. Rep, garden: the thing you do, the thing that grows.</div></div></div>'''
UI=[("isometric","Isometric world","township",4,"Township, Hay Day, Gardenscapes, Forest's forest view","A tilted 3D-looking town or farm you pan around. Everything is on screen at once: buildings, timers, orders, coins, events.","Dense. Needs a lot of art, and the home screen is a shop window. Between sets it is noise.",False),
("topdown","Top-down world","pocketcamp",3,"Stardew Valley, Pocket Camp's garden, Grow a Garden's plots","A plot seen from above, tended square by square. Reads as 'my garden' at a glance; each bed can mean something.","Works at any size, from one bed to a field. Can grow with the client. The strongest fit for a garden that reflects a programme.",True),
("sidescene","Side-on single scene","plantnanny",3,"Plant Nanny, Forest's tree, Flora, Neko Atsume's yard","One plant, one pot, one window. The whole state of play is visible in a second with nothing to pan or tap.","The best glance screen in the set. Weak on freedom and economy: there is little to choose or spend on.",True),
("creature","Creature in a room","finch",2,"Finch, Tamagotchi, Focus Friend","A character who is you, or yours, in a room you decorate. Tasks are a list under the character.","Strongest identification; the room gives freedom and spending. Risk: a sad creature is a mirror, and that cuts both ways.",True),
("map","Map","pokemongo",1,"Pokémon GO, Pikmin Bloom, Zombies, Run!, Flora's Grand Tour","The real world or a route as the board; the activity itself is the input.","Right for walking games, wrong for a gym: the client is standing still, and the map would be the gym.",False),
("minimal","Minimal figure","brainrot",2,"Brainrot, Duolingo's streak screen, Focus Friend's timer","One figure and one number. Nothing to explore; the figure's state is the whole message.","Zero load, instantly read. Too thin on its own for a game that has to last months, but it is the right between-sets layer.",True)]
uitypes=f'''<div class="mono eb">Decision 5 · the screen</div><h2>Six ways to draw a world, side by side.</h2>
<p>Every game in the set uses one of six screen types. The question for RepGarden is which one survives a glance between sets, and which has enough in it to last a twelve-week programme. The screenshots are each game's own store listing.</p>
<div class="grid3" style="gap:4mm 5mm;margin-top:2mm">{"".join(f'<div><div style="display:flex;gap:3mm"><div style="flex:none">{shot(s,n,50)}</div><div><div class="n" style="font-family:Bricolage Grotesque,sans-serif;font-weight:700;font-size:11pt;line-height:1.1">{t}</div><div class="small" style="margin:1mm 0 2mm;line-height:1.3">{ex}</div><div style="font-size:8pt;line-height:1.28;color:#2A3440">{what}</div></div></div><div style="font-size:8pt;line-height:1.28;margin-top:1.5mm;padding-top:1.5mm;border-top:1px solid {LINE}"><span class="tag {"g" if fit else "r"}">{"fits a glance" if fit else "does not fit"}</span> {fit_t}</div></div>' for k,t,s,n,ex,what,fit_t,fit in UI)}</div>
<div class="call" style="margin-top:5mm"><b>Recommendation: two screens, not one.</b> Between sets, a single plant in a single scene, the side-on or minimal type, with one tap. After the workout and in the evening, the garden seen from above, one bed per thing that is growing. The two are the same garden at two distances. Isometric worlds and maps are out: the first is a shop window and the second is for walking.</div>'''
